@ 0 4 0 103f13f7d215cba60f13145e211559d4
+ A field's type must itself be decodable: a type with a decoder of its own, another case class,
+ an enumeration or a sealed trait. One which is none of these is a compile error, and the message
+ names the field, the case class it belongs to and the type which cannot be decoded. A
+ `Vial(label: Text, contents: Poison)`, where `Poison` is an ordinary class, is refused with:

@ 2 0 0 0f842a8362dc3149044451f6022e65f2
+ ```
+ wisteria: the field `contents` of `Vial` needs a `Json.Decodable` instance for `Poison`, but
+ none could be found or derived: `Poison` is not a case class, an enum or a sealed trait; a
+ `Decodable in Text` instance would also serve
+ ```
+
+ Encoding a `Vial` is refused in the same words, for want of a `Json.Encodable`.
+ [Derivation](derivation.md#when-derivation-fails) explains the other ways in which a derivation
+ can fail, and [Decoding](decoding.md) describes the decoders that turn a single text value, such as
+ a JSON string, into a type of one's own.
+
